TOPIC 74 — ASCII vs ISCII vs Unicode
ASCII

American Standard Code for Information Interchange


Originally 7-bit


Represents 128 characters


Mainly designed around English text

ISCII

Indian Script Code for Information Interchange


Developed for representing Indian language scripts.

Unicode
Unicode is a universal character encoding standard designed to represent characters from many writing systems.
UTF-8
Variable-length encoding.
UTF-16
Uses 16-bit code units.
UTF-32
Uses 32-bit code units.
Exam preparation
Remember:
ASCII → mainly basic English characters
ISCII → Indian scripts
Unicode → worldwide character representation
UTF-8/16/32 → Unicode encoding forms

TOPIC 75 — IMPORTANT DIFFERENCES
find() vs index()
find()	index()
Finds first occurrence	Finds first occurrence
Returns -1 if absent	Raises ValueError if absent

find() vs rfind()
find()	rfind()
First occurrence	Last occurrence

split() vs partition()
split()	partition()
Returns list	Returns tuple
Can create many parts	Always 3 parts
Separator removed	Separator retained
Supports maxsplit	No maxsplit argument

upper() vs capitalize() vs title()
Method	Result
upper()	All letters uppercase
capitalize()	First character uppercase
title()	First character of each word uppercase

strip() vs lstrip() vs rstrip()
Method	Removes
strip()	Both sides
lstrip()	Left side
rstrip()	Right side

